pe":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" - If the query is in an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isSuccess"},{"type":"text","value":" state, the data is available via the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" property."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isFetching"},{"type":"text","value":" - In any state, if the query is fetching at any time (including background refetching) "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isFetching"},{"type":"text","value":" will be "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"most"}]},{"type":"text","value":" queries, it's usually sufficient to check for the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isPending"},{"type":"text","value":" state, then the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isError"},{"type":"text","value":" state, then finally, assume that the data is available and render the successful state:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"function Todos() {\n  const { isPending, isError, data, error } = useQuery({\n    queryKey: ['todos'],\n    queryFn: fetchTodoList,\n  })\n\n  if (isPending) {\n    return <span>Loading...</span>\n  }\n\n  if (isError) {\n    return <span>Error: {error.message}</span>\n  }\n\n  // We can assume by this point that `isSuccess === true`\n  return (\n    <ul>\n      {data.map((todo) => (\n        <li key={todo.id}>{todo.title}</li>\n      ))}\n    </ul>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If booleans aren't your thing, you can always use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status"},{"type":"text","value":" state as well:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"function Todos() {\n  const { status, data, error } = useQuery({\n    queryKey: ['todos'],\n    queryFn: fetchTodoList,\n  })\n\n  if (status === 'pending') {\n    return <span>Loading...</span>\n  }\n\n  if (status === 'error') {\n    return <span>Error: {error.message}</span>\n  }\n\n  // also status === 'success', but \"else\" logic works, too\n  return (\n    <ul>\n      {data.map((todo) => (\n        <li key={todo.id}>{todo.title}</li>\n      ))}\n    </ul>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TypeScript will also narrow the type of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" correctly if you've checked for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pending"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"error"},{"type":"text","value":" before accessing it."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"fetchstatus","children":[{"type":"text","value":"FetchStatus"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In addition to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status"},{"type":"text","value":" field, you will also get an additional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetchStatus"},{"type":"text","value":" property with the following options:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetchStatus === 'fetching'"},{"type":"text","value":" - The query is currently fetching."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetchStatus === 'paused'"},{"type":"text","value":" - The query wanted to fetch, but it is paused. Read more about this in the "},{"type":"link","href":"./network-mode.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Network Mode"}]},{"type":"text","value":" guide."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetchStatus === 'idle'"},{"type":"text","value":" - The query is not doing anything at the moment."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"why-two-different-states","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Why two different states?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Background refetches and stale-while-revalidate logic make all combinations for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetchStatus"},{"type":"text","value":" possible. For example:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a query in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"success"},{"type":"text","value":" status will usually be in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"idle"},{"type":"text","value":" fetchStatus, but it could also be in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetching"},{"type":"text","value":" if a background refetch is happening."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a query that mounts and has no data will usually be in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pending"},{"type":"text","value":" status and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetching"},{"type":"text","value":" fetchStatus, but it could also be "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"paused"},{"type":"text","value":" if there is no network connection."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"So keep in mind that a query can be in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pending"},{"type":"text","value":" state without actually fetching data. As a rule of thumb:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status"},{"type":"text","value":" gives information about the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":": Do we have any or not?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetchStatus"},{"type":"text","value":" gives information about the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryFn"},{"type":"text","value":": Is it running or not?"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"further-reading","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Further Reading"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For an alternative way of performing status checks, have a look at "},{"type":"link","href":"https://tkdodo.eu/blog/status-checks-in-preact-query","children":[{"type":"text","value":"this article by TkDodo"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"query-basics","text":"Query Basics","level":2},{"id":"fetchstatus","text":"FetchStatus","level":3},{"id":"why-two-different-states","text":"Why two different states?","level":3},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further Reading","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n## Query Basics\n\nA query is a declarative dependency on an asynchronous source of data that is tied to a **unique key**. A query can be used with any Promise based method (including GET and POST methods) to fetch data from a server. If your method modifies data on the server, we recommend using [Mutations](./mutations.md) instead.\n\n\n\nTo subscribe to a query in your components or custom hooks, call the `useQuery` hook with at least:\n\n\n\n- A **unique key for the query**\n- A function that returns a promise that:\n  - Resolves the data, or\n  - Throws an error\n\n\n\n```tsx\nimport { useQuery } from '@tanstack/preact-query'\n\nfunction App() {\n  const info = useQuery({ queryKey: ['todos'], queryFn: fetchTodoList })\n}\n```\n\n\n\nThe **unique key** you provide is used internally for refetching, caching, and sharing your queries throughout your application.\n\nThe query result returned by `useQuery` contains all of the information about the query that you'll need for templating and any other usage of the data:\n\n\n\n```tsx\nconst result = useQuery({ queryKey: ['todos'], queryFn: fetchTodoList })\n```\n\n\n\nThe `result` object contains a few very important states you'll need to be aware of to be productive. A query can only be in one of the following states at any given moment:\n\n- `isPending` or `status === 'pending'` - The query has no data yet\n- `isError` or `status === 'error'` - The query encountered an error\n- `isSuccess` or `status === 'success'` - The query was successful and data is available\n\nBeyond those primary states, more information is available depending on the state of the query:\n\n- `error` - If the query is in an `isError` state, the error is available via the `error` property.\n- `data` - If the query is in an `isSuccess` state, the data is available via the `data` property.\n- `isFetching` - In any state, if the query is fetching at any time (including background refetching) `isFetching` will be `true`.\n\nFor **most** queries, it's usually sufficient to check for the `isPending` state, then the `isError` state, then finally, assume that the data is available and render the successful state:\n\n\n\n```tsx\nfunction Todos() {\n  const { isPending, isError, data, error } = useQuery({\n    queryKey: ['todos'],\n    queryFn: fetchTodoList,\n  })\n\n  if (isPending) {\n    return <span>Loading...</span>\n  }\n\n  if (isError) {\n    return <span>Error: {error.message}</span>\n  }\n\n  // We can assume by this point that `isSuccess === true`\n  return (\n    <ul>\n      {data.map((todo) => (\n        <li key={todo.id}>{todo.title}</li>\n      ))}\n    </ul>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n\n\nIf booleans aren't your thing, you can always use the `status` state as well:\n\n\n\n```tsx\nfunction Todos() {\n  const { status, data, error } = useQuery({\n    queryKey: ['todos'],\n    queryFn: fetchTodoList,\n  })\n\n  if (status === 'pending') {\n    return <span>Loading...</span>\n  }\n\n  if (status === 'error') {\n    return <span>Error: {error.message}</span>\n  }\n\n  // also status === 'success', but \"else\" logic works, too\n  return (\n    <ul>\n      {data.map((todo) => (\n        <li key={todo.id}>{todo.title}</li>\n      ))}\n    </ul>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n\n\nTypeScript will also narrow the type of `data` correctly if you've checked for `pending` and `error` before accessing it.\n\n### FetchStatus\n\nIn addition to the `status` field, you will also get an additional `fetchStatus` property with the following options:\n\n- `fetchStatus === 'fetching'` - The query is currently fetching.\n- `fetchStatus === 'paused'` - The query wanted to fetch, but it is paused. Read more about this in the [Network Mode](./network-mode.md) guide.\n- `fetchStatus === 'idle'` - The query is not doing anything at the moment.\n\n### Why two different states?\n\nBackground refetches and stale-while-revalidate logic make all combinations for `status` and `fetchStatus` possible. For example:\n\n- a query in `success` status will usually be in `idle` fetchStatus, but it could also be in `fetching` if a background refetch is happening.\n- a query that mounts and has no data will usually be in `pending` status and `fetching` fetchStatus, but it could also be `paused` if there is no network connection.\n\nSo keep in mind that a query can be in `pending` state without actually fetching data. As a rule of thumb:\n\n- The `status` gives information about the `data`: Do we have any or not?\n- The `fetchStatus` gives information about the `queryFn`: Is it running or not?\n\n\n\n## Further Reading\n\nFor an alternative way of performing status checks, have a look at [this article by TkDodo](https://tkdodo.eu/blog/status-checks-in-preact-query).\n\n\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: queries\ntitle: Queries\nref: docs/framework/react/guides/queries.md\nreplace: { 'react-query': 'preact-query', 'React': 'Preact' }\n---\n"}
